TEXT 376 -32 Left 2 ;You can see that overhead lines has smaller capacitive currents\ncompared to the cable. But overhead lines are often much longer\nCurrents:\nThe transient current of the overhead line is appprox. 700A vs cable 3500A\nThe frequency of the transient currents of the overhead line is much higher\nVoltages:\nThe voltage transients are approx. the same height\nThe frequenxy of the transient currents of the overhead line is much higher\nThe voltage drop is due to the network reactance. \nSince the cable current is higher the voltage drop is higher\nSince the overhead current has higher frequencies the voltage drop is higher (XL=omega*L)\nSo the two effects yield to a comparable voltage drop at the network reactance and so \nthe voltage transients are of approx. same height\nComparison 1LC and 50LC variant:\nYou can see that the heights of voltage and current transients are comparable (but not their phase shift!!!)\nThe voltages and currents are of the 50LC type only a little bit higher\nIf the lines length increase the differences increases\nYou can test it by setting the line length of the overhead line (length2) to 60km
